    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Martin Scorsese movies: All 26 Oscar-nominated actors and actresses\ndate: 2024-11-08\n---\nAt age 81, director Martin Scorsese has had the kind of active year that any director half his age would envy. In May 2023, he returned to the Cannes Film Festival (where he triumphed in 1976 with \"Taxi Driver\") to premiere his 27th feature film, \"Killers of the Flower Moon,\" which opened to rapturous reviews for Apple Original Films. The acclaim continued upon the film's 2023 release, and the epic saga went on to earn 10 Academy Award nominations, including Best Picture and nominations for two of the film's stars, Lily Gladstone and Robert De Niro.\n\nOf course, Scorsese is no stranger in directing his cast members to Oscar nominations. In fact, among film directors, the 26 acting nominations that have been earned by actors and actresses in his films are second only to William Wyler, whose cast members received 36 nominations.\n\nLooking back on the performers that have received Oscar nominations for Scorsese films, over 40% of them have been women, belying his reputation as being solely a \"man's director,\" with a track record that includes two memorable wins for Ellen Burstyn and Cate Blanchett.\n\nSo let's take a look back in our photo gallery below at the 26 remarkable performances that were brought to the screen, thanks to the directorial vision of Martin Scorsese.\n\n-\n\nELLEN BURSTYN (Best Actress, \"Alice Doesn't Live Here Anymore\") (1974) (WINNER)\n\nIn a harbinger of things to come, the very first actor to be nominated for a Scorsese film winds up taking the big prize. As a young widow traveling across the country with her son in search of a better life, Ellen Burstyn, on her third nomination, won the Best Actress Oscar with a performance that captures a spirit of optimism that shines thorough the challenges and loneliness that single mothers can sometimes face. Her performance is a testament to Scorsese's enormous skills in getting the best from his female actors.\n\n-\n\nDIANE LADD (Best Supporting Actress, \"Alice Doesn't Live Here Anymore\") (1974)\n\nScorsese scored a second acting nomination for \"Alice\" with Diane Ladd's supporting bid as Flo, the sharp-tongued waitress at Mel and Ruby's Cafe who quickly bonds with Alice after"
