    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Olivia Wilde - Wikipedia\nauthor: Authority control databases\ndate: 2005-01-05\n---\nname: Olivia Wilde\nbirth_name: Olivia Jane Cockburn\nbirth_date: 10 March 1984\nbirth_place: New York City, U.S.\ncitizenship: United States, Ireland\nspouse: Tao Ruspoli (m. 2003)\npartner: * Jason Sudeikis(2011\u20132020)\nworks: Filmography\nyears_active: 2003\u2013present\nchildren: 2\noccupation: Actress, director, producer\nmother: Leslie Cockburn\nfather: Andrew Cockburn\nrelatives: * Claud Cockburn (grandfather), * Sarah Caudwell (aunt), * Alexander Cockburn (uncle), * Patrick Cockburn (uncle)\n\nOlivia Wilde (born Olivia Jane Cockburn, , March 10, 1984) is an American actress and director. She played Remy \"Thirteen\" Hadley on the medical-drama television series House (2007\u20132012), and appeared in the action films Tron: Legacy (2010) and Cowboys & Aliens (2011), the romantic drama film Her (2013), the comedy film The Incredible Burt Wonderstone (2013), and the horror film The Lazarus Effect (2015). She made her Broadway debut playing Julia in 1984 (2017). \n\nWilde made her directorial debut with the teen comedy film Booksmart (2019), which received critical acclaim and won the Independent Spirit Award for Best First Feature. She directed the thriller film Don't Worry Darling (2022), which she also starred in.\n\nEarly life\n\nWilde was born Olivia Jane Cockburn in New York City on March 10, 1984. She grew up in the Georgetown neighborhood of Washington, D.C., while spending summers at Ardmore in Ireland. She attended Georgetown Day School in Washington, D.C., and Phillips Academy in Andover, Massachusetts, graduating in 2002. A dual citizen of the United States and Ireland, she derived her professional surname from Irish author Oscar Wilde, and began using it in high school to honor the writers in her family, many of whom used pen names. She was accepted to Bard College, but deferred her enrollment three times to pursue acting. She then studied at the Gaiety School of Acting in Dublin. For a short time, Wilde's family also had a house in Guilford, Vermont.\n\n"
